# Elisha Bay — /uploads/
#
# Everything under here was written by a member of the public or by a member of
# staff through api/uploads/image.php. It is served to anyone, without a
# session, because product photos and landing images appear on the storefront.
#
# THE RULE THIS FILE EXISTS FOR
# -----------------------------
# An upload directory that can execute PHP is a shell. api/uploads/image.php
# re-encodes every accepted image through GD, which destroys any code riding
# inside the container — but that is one lock, and a directory holding
# attacker-influenced bytes gets more than one.
#
# WHAT DOES NOT WORK HERE, ESTABLISHED BY REQUEST RATHER THAN BY READING
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two obvious ways to write this rule were tried first and both failed. They
# are recorded because each fails in a way that LOOKS like success, which is
# the worst property a security rule can have.
#
#  1. `php_admin_flag engine off`
#     php_admin_* is server-config-only. Apache answers EVERY request in this
#     directory with a blank 500 and logs
#         [core:alert] .../uploads/.htaccess: php_admin_flag not allowed here
#     A directory that 500s reads as "locked down" while in fact being broken —
#     the images simply stop loading, with no obvious cause.
#
#  2. `RemoveHandler .php` + `RemoveType .php` + `AddHandler default-handler .php`
#     Accepted without error, and DOES NOT WORK. Verified: a probe file holding
#     `<?php echo "EXECUTED-".(2*21); ?>` returned `EXECUTED-42` with all three
#     directives in force. XAMPP's httpd-xampp.conf binds PHP with
#     `SetHandler application/x-httpd-php` inside a server-level <FilesMatch>,
#     and SetHandler is unconditional — the extension-to-handler map that
#     Remove/AddHandler edit is never consulted.
#
# What works is overriding that SetHandler at a more specific scope: rule 1
# below. Re-run the probe after any Apache or PHP upgrade. A rule nobody has
# watched fail is not known to work.

# 1. Kill execution. `SetHandler None` at directory scope beats the server-level
#    SetHandler that binds PHP. Verified: the probe file returned its own
#    source, unexecuted, with only this rule in force.
<FilesMatch "\.(?i:php[0-9]?|phtml|phps|pht|phar|cgi|pl|py|rb|sh|exe|dll)$">
    SetHandler None
</FilesMatch>

# 2. Then refuse to serve them at all, so a script that somehow lands here is a
#    403 rather than a source disclosure. Rule 1 stops execution; this stops
#    reading. Each was confirmed working on its own.
<FilesMatch "\.(?i:php[0-9]?|phtml|phps|pht|phar|cgi|pl|py|rb|sh|exe|dll|htaccess)$">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# 3. No CGI, no server-side includes, no directory listing.
Options -ExecCGI -Includes -Indexes

# 4. Uploaded images are named with 32 random hex characters by the server and
#    are never overwritten, so they are safe to cache hard. This deliberately
#    overrides the project root's development no-cache rule, which targets
#    .js/.css/.html only — the two files disagree on purpose.
<FilesMatch "\.(?i:webp|jpe?g|png|gif)$">
    Header set Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
</FilesMatch>

# 5. Never let a browser sniff its way to a content type other than the one we
#    send. With the endpoint emitting nothing but WebP, an uploaded file cannot
#    be coaxed into rendering as HTML.
Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
